{"meta":{"chapter":"02","section":"01","title":"Newton's Second Law","chapter_title":"Kinetics of Particles","course":"dynamics","learning_objectives":["Apply ΣF = ma to particle dynamics","Draw free body diagrams for dynamic problems","Solve problems involving friction, tension, and inclines","Analyze systems with multiple particles"],"concept_ids":["newtons_second_law","kinetic_friction"],"physics_prereqs":["fbd","friction","rectilinear_motion"],"math_prereqs":["algebra","trig"],"units_focus":["N","kg","m/s^2"],"common_errors":["sign","component","mass_weight"],"estimated_time_minutes":60,"prereq_section_ids":["dynamics/01/01","dynamics/01/04"],"practice_ids":["newton_concept_sign","newton_basic_numeric","newton_incline_numeric","newton_friction_steps","newton_tension_numeric","newton_two_force_balance"]},"content":"# Newton's Second Law\n\n> **\"Force is not motion itself. Force is the reason motion changes.\"**\n\n---\n\n## Prerequisites\n\n`fbd` `friction` `rectilinear_motion`\n\n---\n\n## Learning Objectives\n\n1. **Apply** $\\sum \\vec{F} = m\\vec{a}$ to particle dynamics\n2. **Draw** free body diagrams for dynamic problems\n3. **Choose** coordinates that reduce sign mistakes\n4. **Solve** incline, tension, and friction problems with a clean force balance\n\n---\n\n## Opening: From a Stuck Elevator to a Launch Pad\n\nIn 1945 a B-25 bomber struck the Empire State Building, severing the cables of an elevator car with operator Betty Lou Oliver inside. The car fell 75 stories. It did not free-fall the whole way: severed cables piled in the shaft and compressed air beneath the car acted as a crude cushion, so the deceleration at the bottom, though violent, was survivable. Whether someone lives or dies in a fall is rarely about the speed reached; it is about the **net force** during the stop, and net force is exactly what Newton's second law lets an engineer predict and design for. A modern elevator safety brake, an airbag, and a rocket's thrust profile are all engineered around the same equation.\n\nStatics taught us to look for balance. Dynamics starts exactly where balance ends. The moment the net force is not zero, a body changes its velocity. That change might show up as speeding up, slowing down, or turning.\n\nFor engineers, that distinction is practical rather than philosophical:\n\n- a conveyor that jerks products forward has an unbalanced force\n- a drone that pitches upward needs thrust large enough to create acceleration\n- a sled slowing on snow still has motion, but friction is creating a negative acceleration\n\nNewton's second law gives the bridge from a force picture to a motion prediction. It is the single most reused equation in dynamics: every later topic (work-energy, impulse-momentum, vibrations) can be derived from it, so a clean force-to-acceleration workflow is an investment that pays off across the whole course.\n\n## Core Statement\n\n$$\n\\boxed{\\sum \\vec{F} = m \\vec{a}}\n$$\n\nIn component form for plane motion:\n\n$$\n\\sum F_x = ma_x, \\qquad \\sum F_y = ma_y\n$$\n\nThis equation does not say that a single force causes motion. It says the **net** effect of all forces determines acceleration.\n\n### Reading the Equation Carefully\n\nThree subtleties are worth making explicit, because most early mistakes trace back to ignoring one of them.\n\n1. **It is a vector equation.** $\\sum \\vec{F} = m\\vec{a}$ is really three scalar equations bundled together. The force and the acceleration point in the *same* direction, but neither has to point along your motion. A car rounding a curve at constant speed still accelerates (the velocity direction changes), so there must be a net sideways force from the tires.\n2. **Mass is the resistance to acceleration.** Rewriting as $\\vec{a} = \\vec{F}_{net}/m$ shows that for a fixed net force, doubling the mass halves the acceleration. Mass is *inertia*, the quantitative measure of how stubbornly a body resists a change in its motion.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A 2 kg cart and a 6 kg cart feel the same 12 N net force. How do their accelerations compare?\" explanation=\"From a = F_net/m, acceleration is inversely proportional to mass. The 2 kg cart gets 12/2 = 6 m/s^2 while the 6 kg cart gets 12/6 = 2 m/s^2, so the lighter cart accelerates three times as fast under the identical net force.\" options='[{\"text\":\"The 2 kg cart accelerates three times as fast as the 6 kg cart\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Both accelerate equally because the force is the same\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"The 6 kg cart accelerates faster because it has more inertia\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n3. **Only external forces count.** Internal forces between parts of the chosen system cancel in pairs (Newton's third law), so they never appear in the net force of the system as a whole. This is why isolating the right body is the whole game.\n\n### A One-Line Derivation of the Component Form\n\nStart from the vector law and project it onto a fixed orthonormal frame $(\\hat{x}, \\hat{y})$. Writing $\\vec{F} = F_x\\hat{x} + F_y\\hat{y}$ and $\\vec{a} = a_x\\hat{x} + a_y\\hat{y}$, and equating components (since $\\hat{x}$ and $\\hat{y}$ are independent), gives\n\n$$\n\\sum F_x = ma_x, \\qquad \\sum F_y = ma_y.\n$$\n\nNothing is lost in this step; it is simply bookkeeping. The art is choosing the *orientation* of $\\hat{x}$ and $\\hat{y}$ so that the bookkeeping is easy, which is the subject of Step 2 of the workflow below.\n\n## Notation and Sign Convention\n\n| Symbol | Meaning | Units |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\sum F$ | Net force | N |\n| $m$ | Mass | kg |\n| $a$ | Acceleration | m/s^2 |\n| $N$ | Normal force | N |\n| $f$ | Friction force | N |\n\nUse a sign convention that supports the motion you expect to analyze.\n\n- If a block is sliding down an incline, it is often cleanest to choose positive down the slope.\n- If the actual acceleration comes out negative, that does not mean the work is wrong. It means the true acceleration points opposite the axis you assumed.\n- Normal-direction equations often simplify to zero acceleration, even in dynamics, if the particle does not leave the surface.\n\n## The Four-Step Workflow\n\n### 1. Isolate the particle\n\nDraw a free body diagram with only the forces acting on the object.\n\nCommon forces in particle dynamics:\n\n- weight $mg$\n- normal force $N$\n- tension $T$\n- spring force $kx$\n- kinetic friction $f_k = \\mu_k N$\n\n### 2. Choose coordinates with intent\n\nGood coordinates remove clutter. On an incline, use axes parallel and perpendicular to the slope. In pulley problems, align axes with the rope motion.\n\n### 3. Write force balances by direction\n\nYou do not write one giant scalar equation. You write one equation per axis.\n\nTypical pattern:\n\n- along the motion: include the forces that speed up or resist the motion\n- perpendicular to the surface: solve for the normal force first\n\n### 4. Interpret the sign of the answer\n\nA negative acceleration is still useful. It tells you that your assumed positive direction was opposite the true acceleration.\n\n## Assumption Callout: What This Model Is Quietly Assuming\n\nMost particle-dynamics problems in this section rely on several background assumptions:\n\n- the body is modeled as a particle, so rotational effects are ignored,\n- mass is constant,\n- the reference frame is inertial,\n- and laws such as $f_k = \\mu_k N$ are treated as adequate engineering approximations.\n\nThose assumptions are often appropriate, but not universal. If the body tumbles, deforms, experiences strong drag, or has speed-dependent contact behavior, the plain particle model can stop being sufficient.\n\n<dynamic-scenario\n  topic=\"Autonomous Delivery Cart on a Loading Ramp\"\n  context=\"An engineer is tuning the traction control for an autonomous delivery cart that must descend a loading ramp without slipping into the warehouse door. The motor is off, the wheels are free-rolling, and the only forces along the ramp are gravity and kinetic friction.\"\n  course=\"dynamics\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Common Reasoning Traps\n\n- treating weight as if it acts along the incline instead of vertically downward\n- forgetting that friction opposes relative motion, not necessarily the axis you prefer\n- using $\\sum F = 0$ out of habit from statics\n- solving for acceleration before finding the normal force\n\n## Worked Example\n\n### Example 2.1: Block on a Rough Incline\n\n**Problem:** A 10 kg block slides down a $30^\\circ$ incline with $\\mu_k = 0.2$. Find its acceleration.\n\n### Step A: Draw the forces\n\n- Weight: $W = mg = 98.1$ N downward\n- Normal force: $N$ perpendicular to the surface\n- Friction: $f_k = \\mu_k N$ up the incline because the block slides down\n\n### Step B: Resolve the weight\n\nAlong the incline:\n\n$$\nW_{\\parallel} = mg\\sin 30^\\circ = 49.05 \\text{ N}\n$$\n\nPerpendicular to the incline:\n\n$$\nW_{\\perp} = mg\\cos 30^\\circ = 84.9 \\text{ N}\n$$\n\n### Step C: Use the perpendicular equation first\n\nBecause the block stays on the surface,\n\n$$\n\\sum F_{\\perp} = 0 \\Rightarrow N - mg\\cos 30^\\circ = 0\n$$\n\nSo,\n\n$$\nN = 84.9 \\text{ N}\n$$\n\n### Step D: Compute friction\n\n$$\nf_k = \\mu_k N = 0.2(84.9) = 17.0 \\text{ N}\n$$\n\n### Step E: Apply Newton's second law along the incline\n\nTaking positive down the incline,\n\n$$\n\\sum F_{\\parallel} = ma\n$$\n\n$$\nmg\\sin 30^\\circ - f_k = ma\n$$\n\n$$\n49.05 - 17.0 = 10a\n$$\n\n$$\na = 3.21 \\text{ m/s}^2\n$$\n\nThe block accelerates down the incline because gravity's downhill component is still larger than friction.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Predict Each Step of the Rough-Incline Solution\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"For the 10 kg block on the 30 degree incline, what is the normal force N? (g = 9.81 m/s^2)\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"N = mg = 98.1 N\",\"N = mg*cos(30) = 84.9 N\",\"N = mg*sin(30) = 49.05 N\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Since the block stays on the surface, the perpendicular balance gives N = mg*cos(30) = 98.1 * 0.866 = 84.9 N. Only the cosine component of weight presses into the slope.\"},{\"prompt\":\"With mu_k = 0.2, what is the kinetic friction force?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"f_k = mu_k*N = 17.0 N\",\"f_k = mu_k*mg = 19.6 N\",\"f_k = mu_k*mg*sin(30) = 9.81 N\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"Friction depends on the normal force, not the full weight: f_k = mu_k*N = 0.2 * 84.9 = 17.0 N, acting up the slope because the block slides down.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Taking positive down the slope, which equation gives the acceleration?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"mg*sin(30) - f_k = ma\",\"mg - f_k = ma\",\"mg*cos(30) - f_k = ma\"],\"answerIndex\":0,\"reveal\":\"Along the slope only the sine component of weight drives the motion and friction opposes it: 49.05 - 17.0 = 10a, so a = 3.21 m/s^2.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Big idea: always solve the perpendicular (normal-force) equation first, because f_k = mu_k*N cannot be computed until N is known. Then apply F = ma along the slope.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\n## A Quick Check for Physical Sense\n\nBefore moving on, ask:\n\n- Is the acceleration smaller than $g$? Yes, because only part of gravity acts along the ramp.\n- Is the acceleration smaller than the frictionless value $g\\sin 30^\\circ$? Yes, because friction reduces it.\n- Would the answer change if the block were moving upward? Yes. Friction would reverse direction.\n\nThat short sense check catches many setup mistakes before they become algebra mistakes.\n\n### Example 2.2: Connected Blocks Over a Pulley\n\n**Problem:** A 4 kg block hangs from a light cord that passes over a frictionless, massless pulley at the edge of a table and connects to a 6 kg block resting on the horizontal table top. The kinetic friction coefficient between the 6 kg block and the table is $\\mu_k = 0.25$. The system is released from rest. Find the acceleration of the system and the tension in the cord.\n\nThis is the first example where the body of interest is **two particles linked by a constraint**. The cord forces the blocks to share the same speed and the same acceleration magnitude, $a$, at every instant.\n\n**Step A: Isolate each block (two FBDs).**\n\n- Hanging block (4 kg): weight $m_1 g$ downward, tension $T$ upward. It accelerates *down* at $a$.\n- Table block (6 kg): weight $m_2 g$ down, normal force $N$ up, tension $T$ pulling toward the pulley (horizontal), kinetic friction $f_k$ opposing its motion (pointing away from the pulley). It accelerates *toward the pulley* at $a$.\n\n**Step B: Vertical equation for the table block (find $N$ first).**\n\n$$\n\\sum F_y = 0 \\Rightarrow N - m_2 g = 0 \\Rightarrow N = (6)(9.81) = 58.86 \\text{ N}\n$$\n\n$$\nf_k = \\mu_k N = 0.25(58.86) = 14.71 \\text{ N}\n$$\n\n**Step C: Newton's second law for each block (positive = direction of motion).**\n\nHanging block (positive down):\n\n$$\nm_1 g - T = m_1 a\n$$\n\nTable block (positive toward pulley):\n\n$$\nT - f_k = m_2 a\n$$\n\n**Step D: Add the two equations to eliminate $T$.**\n\n$$\nm_1 g - f_k = (m_1 + m_2)\\,a\n$$\n\n$$\na = \\frac{m_1 g - \\mu_k m_2 g}{m_1 + m_2} = \\frac{39.24 - 14.71}{10} = \\boxed{2.45 \\text{ m/s}^2}\n$$\n\n**Step E: Back-substitute for the tension.**\n\n$$\nT = m_1 (g - a) = 4(9.81 - 2.45) = \\boxed{29.43 \\text{ N}}\n$$\n\n**Sanity checks.** The tension is less than the hanging weight ($29.43 < 39.24$ N); it must be, otherwise the 4 kg block could not accelerate downward. Verifying with the other block, $T = m_2 a + f_k = 6(2.45) + 14.71 = 29.43$ N, which matches. Adding equations to cancel the internal cord force is the standard trick for any connected-particle system.\n\n### Example 2.3: Block Pushed Up an Incline by a Horizontal Force\n\n**Problem:** A 20 kg block is pushed *up* a $25^\\circ$ incline by a horizontal force $P = 200$ N. The block is already sliding upward and $\\mu_k = 0.15$. Find the acceleration.\n\nThis example shows why solving the normal-force equation first is not optional: here the applied force itself changes $N$, so friction cannot be computed until $N$ is known.\n\n**Step A: Choose incline-aligned axes.** Let $x$ point up the slope, $y$ perpendicular to the surface. Resolve every force into these axes. The horizontal push $P$ has a component $P\\cos 25^\\circ$ up the slope and a component $P\\sin 25^\\circ$ pressing *into* the surface. Gravity has $mg\\sin 25^\\circ$ down the slope and $mg\\cos 25^\\circ$ into the surface.\n\n**Step B: Perpendicular equation (no acceleration off the surface).**\n\n$$\n\\sum F_y = 0 \\Rightarrow N - mg\\cos 25^\\circ - P\\sin 25^\\circ = 0\n$$\n\n$$\nN = (20)(9.81)\\cos 25^\\circ + 200\\sin 25^\\circ = 177.82 + 84.52 = 262.34 \\text{ N}\n$$\n\nNotice $N$ is larger than the weight component alone, because the horizontal push also pins the block to the slope. A naive $N = mg\\cos\\theta$ would underestimate friction badly.\n\n**Step C: Friction.** The block slides up, so kinetic friction acts down the slope:\n\n$$\nf_k = \\mu_k N = 0.15(262.34) = 39.35 \\text{ N}\n$$\n\n**Step D: Newton's second law along the slope (positive up).**\n\n$$\nP\\cos 25^\\circ - mg\\sin 25^\\circ - f_k = ma\n$$\n\n$$\n181.26 - 82.92 - 39.35 = 20a\n$$\n\n$$\na = \\frac{58.99}{20} = \\boxed{2.95 \\text{ m/s}^2}\n$$\n\n**Interpretation.** The block accelerates up the slope because the driving component of the push ($181.26$ N) exceeds gravity-along-slope plus friction ($82.92 + 39.35 = 122.27$ N). If the push were removed, the same block would decelerate and reverse, and friction would then flip to point up the slope; the FBD must be redrawn for that phase.\n\n## Engineering Trade-Off: Force Detail Versus Analysis Speed\n\nForce-based analysis gives excellent local insight, but it becomes more demanding as realism increases.\n\nAn engineer may need to decide whether to include:\n\n- rolling resistance separately from sliding friction,\n- aerodynamic drag,\n- wheel or pulley inertia,\n- or compliance in the support structure.\n\nEach added effect improves fidelity, but it also increases setup complexity and parameter uncertainty. In early-stage design, a lean Newton's-law model may be the right tool for bounding acceleration and traction. In final verification, the same system may need a richer model or experimental correlation.\n\n## Sensitivity Analysis: How Much Does Friction Matter?\n\nFor the same 10 kg block on a $30^\\circ$ incline, the acceleration can be written compactly as:\n\n$$\na = g(\\sin 30^\\circ - \\mu_k \\cos 30^\\circ)\n$$\n\nThat form makes the parameter sensitivity visible immediately.\n\n| $\\mu_k$ | Predicted Acceleration |\n| ---: | ---: |\n| 0.10 | 4.06 m/s² |\n| 0.20 | 3.21 m/s² |\n| 0.30 | 2.36 m/s² |\n| 0.40 | 1.51 m/s² |\n\nThis is not just algebra. A change of 0.1 in friction coefficient shifts the predicted acceleration by roughly 0.85 m/s² in this example. For a docking ramp, stop gate, or product-handling line, that difference can be operationally significant.\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"Sliding Down a Rough Incline: a = g(sin(theta) - mu_k*cos(theta))\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"theta\",\"label\":\"Incline angle\",\"min\":0,\"max\":60,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"deg\",\"default\":30},{\"name\":\"mu_k\",\"label\":\"Kinetic friction coefficient\",\"min\":0,\"max\":0.8,\"step\":0.05,\"unit\":\"\",\"default\":0.2},{\"name\":\"g\",\"label\":\"Gravity\",\"min\":1.6,\"max\":9.81,\"step\":0.01,\"unit\":\"m/s^2\",\"default\":9.81}],\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"Weight component along slope per unit mass\",\"unit\":\"m/s^2\",\"expr\":\"g*sin(theta*3.14159265/180)\"},{\"label\":\"Friction deceleration per unit mass\",\"unit\":\"m/s^2\",\"expr\":\"mu_k*g*cos(theta*3.14159265/180)\"},{\"label\":\"Net acceleration down slope\",\"unit\":\"m/s^2\",\"expr\":\"g*sin(theta*3.14159265/180) - mu_k*g*cos(theta*3.14159265/180)\"}],\"plot\":{\"x\":\"theta\",\"output\":2}}'></parameter-explorer>\n\nA negative net acceleration in the explorer means friction wins: the downhill weight component is too small to overcome friction, so a block at rest would not start sliding (and a moving block would decelerate).\n\n## Assumption Breakdown Case: When Constant Friction Stops Being Trustworthy\n\nThe simple incline model assumes kinetic friction can be represented by a constant $\\mu_k$. That assumption starts to weaken when:\n\n- speed changes the contact behavior,\n- surface heating changes the interface,\n- contamination or moisture alters the traction law,\n- or deformation makes rolling resistance more relevant than sliding friction.\n\nAt that point, the model may still be useful as a first estimate, but it stops being a high-confidence predictor of exact stopping distance or controller response. That is usually where engineers transition from closed-form analysis to test-backed parameter identification.\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\n<interactive-quiz\n  question=\"A crate is moving up an incline but slowing down. Which direction does kinetic friction act?\"\n  conceptId=\"friction\"\n  options='[{\"text\":\"Up the incline because the crate is moving up.\",\"isCorrect\":false},{\"text\":\"Down the incline because friction opposes the relative motion.\",\"isCorrect\":true},{\"text\":\"Perpendicular to the incline.\",\"isCorrect\":false},{\"text\":\"It depends on the mass only.\",\"isCorrect\":false}]'\n  explanation=\"Correct. Kinetic friction always opposes the direction of sliding. If the crate is moving up the incline, friction points down the incline.\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n<interactive-quiz\n  question=\"Why do many incline problems use axes parallel and perpendicular to the surface?\"\n  conceptId=\"newtons_second_law\"\n  options='[{\"text\":\"Because gravity becomes larger in those axes.\",\"isCorrect\":false},{\"text\":\"Because the normal force and acceleration constraints become easier to express.\",\"isCorrect\":true},{\"text\":\"Because Newton’s second law only works in rotated axes.\",\"isCorrect\":false}]'\n  explanation=\"Exactly. Rotating the axes does not change the physics, but it often simplifies the equations because the normal force lies entirely in one direction and the motion often lies in the other.\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Engineering Applications\n\nThe force-to-acceleration workflow scales from a single block to systems worth millions of dollars. The physics does not change; only the force inventory grows.\n\n| Application | Driving Force | Resisting Force | What the Engineer Sizes |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Elevator hoist motor | Cable tension above $mg$ | Weight, cabin inertia | Motor torque for target acceleration and a safe jerk profile |\n| Automotive launch | Tire traction $\\mu_s N$ | Drag, rolling resistance, grade | Maximum acceleration before wheel slip |\n| Conveyor / pick-and-place | Belt friction or actuator force | Part inertia, friction | Force needed to index parts without slipping |\n| Rocket first stage | Thrust above $mg$ | Weight (falling), drag | Thrust-to-weight ratio for liftoff acceleration |\n| Funicular / inclined lift | Haul cable tension | Gravity along grade, friction | Cable force for controlled ascent on a grade |\n\n> **Engineering in Action:** A car's maximum launch acceleration is capped not by engine power but by $a_{max} = \\mu_s g$ for a flat road, the same $\\mu N$ friction limit used on the incline block. That is why traction (tire compound, downforce) often matters more than horsepower off the line.\n\n## Summary\n\n### Key Equations\n\n| Concept | Equation |\n| --- | --- |\n| Newton's second law (vector) | $\\sum \\vec{F} = m\\vec{a}$ |\n| Component form (plane motion) | $\\sum F_x = ma_x$, $\\sum F_y = ma_y$ |\n| Kinetic friction | $f_k = \\mu_k N$ |\n| Incline acceleration (sliding down, no applied force) | $a = g(\\sin\\theta - \\mu_k \\cos\\theta)$ |\n| Connected blocks (one hanging, one on rough table) | $a = \\dfrac{m_1 g - \\mu_k m_2 g}{m_1 + m_2}$ |\n\n### Key Takeaways\n\n- Newton's second law connects net force to acceleration, not directly to velocity.\n- It is a vector equation: write separate scalar equations, one per axis.\n- In incline problems, solve the normal-force equation before the friction equation, because $f_k = \\mu_k N$ depends on $N$ (and an applied force can change $N$).\n- For connected particles, add the per-block equations to cancel the internal cord tension, then back-substitute.\n- Friction opposes the direction of *sliding*, so its sign flips when the motion reverses; redraw the FBD for each phase of motion.\n- Use sign and magnitude checks to verify the answer makes physical sense.\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"newtons_second_law\",\"kinetic_friction\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## What's Next?\n\nIn **Section 2.2: Work and Energy**, we will solve some of the same motion problems using energy bookkeeping instead of direct force-acceleration equations.\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"newton_concept_sign","type":"multiple_choice","stem":"A cart moves to the right while slowing down on a horizontal track. If right is positive, what must be true about its acceleration?","options":["It is positive because the cart is moving right.","It is negative because acceleration opposes the motion.","It is zero because the cart is still moving.","Its sign depends only on the cart's mass."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Velocity describes direction of motion. Acceleration describes how velocity is changing. If the cart is moving right but slowing down, acceleration points left and is negative.","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["newtons_second_law"],"misconception_id":"sign_of_acceleration"},{"id":"newton_basic_numeric","type":"numeric","stem":"A 5 kg object experiences a net horizontal force of 20 N. Find its acceleration.","final_answer":{"value":4,"unit":"m/s^2","tolerance":0.1},"difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["newtons_second_law"]},{"id":"newton_incline_numeric","type":"numeric","stem":"A 15 kg block slides down a frictionless 40 degree incline. Find its acceleration.","final_answer":{"value":6.31,"unit":"m/s^2","tolerance":0.1},"difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["newtons_second_law"]},{"id":"newton_friction_steps","type":"step_based","stem":"A 20 kg crate is pushed with an 80 N horizontal force on a floor with mu_k = 0.3. Find the acceleration.","steps":[{"step_id":1,"description":"Find the normal force N = mg.","expected_value":196.2,"unit":"N","tolerance":1},{"step_id":2,"description":"Find the kinetic friction force f_k = mu_k N.","expected_value":58.9,"unit":"N","tolerance":1},{"step_id":3,"description":"Find the net horizontal force and divide by mass.","expected_value":1.06,"unit":"m/s^2","tolerance":0.1}],"final_answer":{"value":1.06,"unit":"m/s^2","tolerance":0.1},"difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["newtons_second_law","kinetic_friction"]},{"id":"newton_tension_numeric","type":"numeric","stem":"A 12 kg sled is pulled horizontally by a 36 N rope on frictionless ice. Find the acceleration.","final_answer":{"value":3,"unit":"m/s^2","tolerance":0.1},"difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["newtons_second_law"]},{"id":"newton_two_force_balance","type":"multiple_choice","stem":"Which statement best explains why the normal-force equation for a sliding block on an incline is often written as sum F_perp = 0?","options":["Because Newton's second law does not apply perpendicular to the surface.","Because the block usually has no acceleration through the surface.","Because the normal force is always equal to the weight.","Because friction acts only perpendicular to the surface."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Even in dynamics, acceleration can be zero in one direction. For a block constrained to remain on the surface, the perpendicular acceleration is often zero, which makes the normal-force equation much simpler.","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["newtons_second_law"],"misconception_id":"sumf_zero_habit"}]}}